From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yevhen Salitrynskyi <ysalitrynskyi@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 01:56:48 -0400
Subject: turn drag-to-Space on, once the part that could lose a tab is tested

This shipped off, on the grounds that a drop handler owns the tab it is given
and this one had never had a real drag through it. That was the right caution
and the wrong conclusion: the dangerous part does not need a drag to test.

HandleTabDrop now takes the first dragged tab rather than reading
DragSessionData to count them, which is what made it untestable -- a session
cannot be built outside a real drag. Two tests with a fake drag controller cover
it: dropping on a Space keeps the tab in the strip, moves it to that Space and
makes that Space active; dropping on nothing leaves the tab alone.

What is still not covered is Chromium's own decision to consult the target
mid-drag. If that never fires, the drag does nothing, which is where this
started.

The switcher is now a pointer rather than a reference, because the target only
uses it to hit-test a screen point and a caller that already knows the Space has
no switcher to give. The first version of the test passed a null reference,
which is undefined behaviour whether or not it is read.

Why: "I cannot verify this" was true of the drag and false of the code.
Removable when: never, short of dropping Spaces.
